<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oireachtas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oireachtas Éireann</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oireachtas is the national parliament of Ireland, made up of the President and two houses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil Éireann</a> and Seanad Éireann. It was established under the Constitution of 1937, following the parliament of the Irish Free State. The Dáil, elected by proportional representation, chooses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoiseach">Taoiseach</a> and is the more powerful chamber.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oireachtas.jpg" alt="Oireachtas" width="320" height="281" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lough Swilly · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:34th_D%C3%A1il_Seating_Plan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word oireachtas comes from the Irish airecht or oireacht, a deliberative assembly of freemen, ultimately from airig, a freeman. Its first recorded use as the name of a legislative body was within the Irish Free State. Earlier Irish parliaments are treated as its predecessors.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Parliament of Ireland</a> was founded in the thirteenth century as the legislature of the lordship of Ireland and lasted until 1801. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poynings-law">Poynings' Law</a> in 1494 until its repeal in 1782 it was subordinate to the Parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The Constitution of 1782 gave it greater independence, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-grattan">Henry Grattan</a> it was known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grattans-parliament">Grattan's Parliament</a>. In 1800 it approved its own abolition by the Act of Union, in force from 1 January 1801.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The next legislature was the unicameral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil Éireann</a>, set up by Irish republicans in 1919. In 1920 the British government created a home rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-southern-ireland">Parliament of Southern Ireland</a>, which most Irish politicians boycotted and which was abolished in 1922. The Oireachtas of the Irish Free State then took shape under its constitution. It consisted of the King, represented by a Governor-General, and two houses, the Dáil and the Seanad. The Free State Senate was abolished in May 1936, and the role of the monarch was removed in December 1936. The modern Oireachtas came into being on 29 December 1937, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-ireland">Constitution of Ireland</a> was adopted.</p><p>Radio broadcasts of the Oireachtas began with ceremonial addresses, the first being that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a> during his 1963 state visit. Regular radio broadcasting of edited proceedings began in October 1986. Television coverage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil</a>, Seanad and committees began in 1990, 1991 and 1993 respectively. The Houses of the Oireachtas Commission, set up by statute in 2003, employs the support staff of both houses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Oireachtas consists of the President of Ireland and two houses, which sit in Leinster House in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, an eighteenth-century ducal palace. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil</a> has greater powers. It is elected by proportional representation by means of a single transferable vote, and the Seanad has 60 members: 11 nominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoiseach">Taoiseach</a>, 6 elected by university constituencies and 43 from vocational panels.</p><p>A bill must pass the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil</a> and, in most circumstances, the Seanad, though the Dáil can override a Seanad refusal after 180 days, and then be signed by the president. Amendments to the constitution must also be put to a referendum. The Oireachtas may not enact a law providing for the death penalty, and a law is invalid to the extent that it conflicts with the constitution.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oireachtas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Irish translation by Liam Ó Rinn, later chief translator of the <b>Oireachtas</b>, was printed in the Freeman's Journal on 3 April 1923 and in the Defence Forces' magazine An tÓglach that November; it may date from as early as 1917.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amhran-na-bhfiann">Amhrán na bhFiann</a></li><li><blockquote>It is a parliamentary democracy with the <b>Oireachtas</b> and a Taoiseach.</blockquote><a href="/state/ireland">Ireland</a></li></ul></section>
